        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Arithmetic: 14,161 / 5,76,793 = 2.5%; 2,736 / 14,161 = 19.3%; 958 / 2,736 = 35.0%; 958 / 5,76,793 = 0.17% (about 1 in 600); 958 / 9,37,876 = 0.10% (about 1 in 980).
  </p>

  <p>
    The steepest cut is the first: about 97 in 100 who sit prelims never reach the mains. That is where a parallel skill or job matters most.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take: two stages of competition</p>
    <p>
      Getting in is the first filter. UPSC alone has a prelims, a mains and an interview. Even after selection, service allocation and posting are ranked again. Clearing one round does not shrink the next.
    </p>
    <p>
      Arts graduates are not at a disadvantage here. Humanities graduates were about 29% of selected candidates in 2023-24, and 84.1% of optional subjects chosen by selected candidates were humanities, per <a href="https://theprint.in/feature/engineers-53-per-cent-upsc-selections/2927230/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ThePrint's reading of the UPSC report</a>. The subject helps; the base rate still stands.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Run the exam with an income:</strong> you can prepare for SSC CGL or bank exams while working, and it keeps a safety net under you. Read <a href="/blog/government-jobs/government-exam-preparation-while-working-india/">how to prepare while working</a> and <a href="/blog/government-jobs/ssc-vs-bank-exams-which-to-prepare-india/">SSC vs bank exams</a> before choosing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    If an attempt does not work out, <a href="/blog/government-jobs/what-to-do-after-failed-upsc-attempts-india/">what to do after failed UPSC attempts</a> and <a href="/blog/career-options/upsc-alternative-careers-for-humanities-students-india/">UPSC alternative careers for humanities students</a> map the next moves.
  </p>

