  </p>

  <h2 id="four-year">3-year or 4-year BA: what changes</h2>

  <p>
    Under the National Education Policy, many universities now offer a four-year honours degree with research. It changes a few doors.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>UGC NET:</strong> for Assistant Professor eligibility needs a master's with 55% marks (50% for reserved categories). Four-year degree holders with an aggregate of at least 75% can appear for JRF and PhD admission, not for Assistant Professor eligibility. Check the <a href="https://ugcnet.nta.nic.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NTA UGC NET portal</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>B.Ed:</strong> the proposed one-year B.Ed (draft NCTE rules) is for four-year degree holders and postgraduates. Three-year degree holders continue with the two-year course.</li>
    <li><strong>Exams like UPSC, SSC and banking:</strong> any bachelor's degree qualifies, whether three or four years.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A three-year BA is still fully valid. The four-year version helps mainly if you plan to do research or teach.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skill-stack">The skill layer that raises your pay</h2>

  <p>
    Whichever lane you pick, the ceiling is set by your <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>: one core skill, one multiplier skill and proof the market can trust. For a BA graduate, the core is usually writing, research or communication. The multiplier is where income jumps, and where earlier financial freedom starts to look realistic.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Data basics:</strong> Excel or Google Sheets, then SQL and one dashboard tool. This turns a humanities graduate into a candidate for analyst roles.</li>
    <li><strong>AI-assisted research, with verification:</strong> use AI to summarise and draft, then check every fact. Employers pay for the person who catches the wrong number.</li>
    <li><strong>Spoken and written English:</strong> a multiplier across every lane, from interviews to client emails. Work on it daily in small sessions.</li>
    <li><strong>Selling and clear offers:</strong> knowing how to explain what you do and ask for work is what later makes freelancing and consulting possible.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Start now, then deepen later. Right now, use AI to speed up research and drafting while you check every claim yourself. As tools mature and you gain domain depth, move towards designing repeatable workflows for a team or client, such as a weekly market brief or a reporting template. That is how one person's output grows without a promise of any fixed multiplier.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start with open learning: YouTube tutorials, official documentation, and SWAYAM, the government platform with courses from NPTEL, IGNOU, UGC and others. Judge a course by its instructor, syllabus depth, update date and whether it ends with a project. Certificates alone are weak proof.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take on AI</p>
    <p>
      Routine drafting, basic summaries and first-pass content are being automated, which squeezes entry-level writing roles. Interviewing, verifying facts, taste, negotiation and relationships are not. If your BA path depends only on producing routine text, add the human layer now.
    </p>
  </div>

