  <h2 id="cost-of-waiting">The cost of waiting, in rupees</h2>
  <figure class="article-visual"><img src="/images/blog/career-options/ba-career-options/ba-career-options-stat-cost-waiting-rupees.webp" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Stat panel showing the salary opportunity cost of waiting one or two years before taking a first offer" title="The cost of waiting, in rupees" /><figcaption>Waiting feels free. It is not: a delayed first offer carries a real salary opportunity cost.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>
    Waiting feels free. It is not. Take a simple example, not a quote: if a first offer would be Rs 20,000 a month, every year outside work forgoes Rs 2.4 lakh in salary, before any coaching fee, course fee or living cost. Two years is close to Rs 5 lakh.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is why a paid role with a parallel plan often beats a full-time gap. The job gives you savings, proof and a reference. The parallel plan (an exam, a course, a portfolio) keeps the bigger goal alive.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>A safer sequence for many BA graduates:</strong> take an entry job in an adjacent field, build the skill stack alongside it, and prepare for one exam or course that your income can support. Pivot when the proof, not the pressure, says it is time.
    </p>
  </div>

